= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the integral normalization of the <Chern connection> curvature. For a <holomorphic local frame> $e$, put $h_e=h(e,e)>0$. The <fundamental form of a Hermitian holomorphic line bundle> is
$$
\boxed{\omega_{(\mathcal L,h)}=\frac{i}{2\pi}F_h
=-\frac{i}{2\pi}\partial\bar\partial\log h_e.}
$$
This follows from the <local formula for the Chern connection on a line bundle>, $F_h=\bar\partial\partial\log h_e$. It is a closed <real (1, 1)-form> representing the <first Chern class>. If $h_e=e^{-\varphi}$, the formula becomes $(i/(2\pi))\partial\bar\partial\varphi$.

The <Hermitian metric> is positive when this is a <positive real (1, 1)-form>: for every nonzero $v\in T^{1,0}M$,
$$
\boxed{-i\omega_{(\mathcal L,h)}(v,\bar v)>0.}
$$
Equivalently, the matrix $(\partial^2\varphi/\partial z_j\partial\bar z_k)$ is positive definite in every frame. Such a metric makes $\mathcal L$ a <positive holomorphic line bundle>. Omitting the normalization factor $2\pi$ changes none of these positivity conditions.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply <Serre duality for compact complex manifolds> to $\mathcal L\otimes K_M$, where $K_M$ is the <canonical bundle>:
$$
H^n(M,\mathcal L\otimes K_M)^*\cong H^0(M,\mathcal L^{-1}).
$$
We can show the group on the right vanishes directly from the embedding. Since $\mathcal L^{-1}=i^*\mathcal O(-1)$, the <tautological bundle> inclusion gives an inclusion of <holomorphic vector bundles>
$$
\mathcal L^{-1}\hookrightarrow M\times\mathbb C^{r+1}.
$$
A global <holomorphic section> of $\mathcal L^{-1}$ consequently determines $r+1$ global <holomorphic functions> on $M$. Compactness and the <maximum modulus principle> make these functions constant on every connected component. On such a component the section is therefore a constant vector $v$ lying in the tautological line $i(x)$ for every $x$.

If $v\ne0$, all the points $i(x)$ equal $[v]$. This contradicts the fact that $i$ is an embedding of a component of positive dimension $n\geq1$. Hence $v=0$ on every component, and $H^0(M,\mathcal L^{-1})=0$. Duality proves
$$
\boxed{H^n(M,\mathcal L\otimes K_M)=0.}
$$

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Dolbeault theorem> identifies $H^2(M,\mathcal O_M)$ with $H^{0,2}_{\bar\partial}(M)$. It vanishes by hypothesis, and complex conjugation in the <Hodge decomposition theorem for compact Kähler manifolds> makes $H^{2,0}$ vanish too. Thus every real degree-two cohomology class has type $(1,1)$.

Let $\omega$ be a Kähler form. Choose a rational cohomology class $\eta$ sufficiently close to $[\omega]$. More concretely, using harmonic representatives for a fixed <Kähler metric>, the harmonic form representing $\eta$ is close to $\omega$ in every smooth norm: harmonic forms constitute a finite-dimensional space. It is a real closed $(1,1)$ form, and is positive if sufficiently close to $\omega$, by compactness. This is the openness of the <Kähler cone>. Multiplying by a positive integer gives an integral Kähler class $\kappa=N\eta$.

The <holomorphic exponential sequence> contains the cohomology segment
$$
H^1(M,\mathcal O_M^*)\xrightarrow{c_1}H^2(M,\mathbb Z)\longrightarrow H^2(M,\mathcal O_M)=0.
$$
Choose an integral lift of $\kappa$. Exactness gives a <holomorphic line bundle> $\mathcal L$ having that <first Chern class>. We must ensure that it has a positive metric, rather than merely a positive representative of its class.

Choose any <Hermitian metric> $h_0$ and let $\theta_0$ be its <normalized curvature form of a Hermitian holomorphic line bundle>. If $\theta$ is the positive form representing $\kappa$, then $\theta-\theta_0$ is an exact real $(1,1)$ form. The <ddbar lemma> gives a real smooth function $u$ with
$$
\theta-\theta_0=\frac{i}{2\pi}\partial\bar\partial u.
$$
Replace $h_0$ by $h=e^{-u}h_0$. Its normalized curvature is $\theta$, so $\mathcal L$ is a <positive holomorphic line bundle>. The <Kodaira embedding theorem> now applies: sufficiently many sections of a sufficiently high tensor power define the desired embedding. Therefore
$$
\boxed{H^2(M,\mathcal O_M)=0\ \Longrightarrow\ M\text{ admits a holomorphic embedding into complex projective space}.}
$$

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the <Calabi-Yau threefold> in the stated convention, the <Hodge decomposition theorem for compact Kähler manifolds> gives
$$
0=H^1(M,\mathbb C)=H^{1,0}_{\bar\partial}(M)\oplus H^{0,1}_{\bar\partial}(M).
$$
In particular, the <Dolbeault theorem> yields $H^1(M,\mathcal O_M)=0$. By <Serre duality for compact complex manifolds> in dimension three,
$$
H^2(M,\mathcal O_M)^*\cong H^1(M,K_M).
$$
The <canonical bundle> is trivial, so the group on the right equals $H^1(M,\mathcal O_M)=0$. Thus $H^2(M,\mathcal O_M)=0$, and part (c) gives
$$
\boxed{\text{Every Calabi-Yau threefold satisfying the given definition is projective}.}
$$

